WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.330
We will now talk about how
biologists study gene expression

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.930
to analyze genes implicated
in the diauxic shift.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.190
Let's make a simple experiment.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:17.080
Let's measure the expression of
three genes at seven checkpoints

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.830
before and after the diauxic shift.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:24.730
And each element in the resulting
three-by-seven matrix

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.530
is the expression level of
gene i at checkpoint j.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.300
What is the expression level?

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.160
Well, one gene in a genome may generate

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.540
many transcripts, while another
may generate few transcripts.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:43.070
Expression level is a relative
measurement of the number of transcripts

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.650
in the cell.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.029
And, we can visualize this

00:00:50.029 --> 00:00:54.088
7-mer expression vector as
a plot on seven points,

00:00:54.088 --> 00:00:59.059
and looking at this plot, you can
immediately see that the blue gene most

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:02.401
likely has nothing to do
with the diauxic shift

00:01:02.401 --> 00:01:08.940
because the expression of this gene doesn't
change before and after the diauxic shift.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.570
However, the green gene mostly likely

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.240
is implicated in the diauxic shift
because its expression increases,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:22.088
and the red gene is also most likely
implicated in the diauxic shift

00:01:22.088 --> 00:01:25.568
because its expression is decreased.

00:01:25.568 --> 00:01:31.065
Instead of analyzing expression levels
directly, biologists often prefer

00:01:31.065 --> 00:01:36.475
to study the logarithm of expression level,
and we will follow this approach.

00:01:36.475 --> 00:01:41.325
Below this line, you see expression
level represented as logarithm.

00:01:42.395 --> 00:01:46.985
We just need to remember that positive
expression levels, after transforming

00:01:46.985 --> 00:01:52.030
to logarithm, correspond to an
increase in the expression of gene,

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.380
and negative expression levels,
after transforming to logarithm,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.074
correspond to a decrease
in the gene expression.

00:01:59.074 --> 00:02:05.630
And here's a little bit larger
ten-by-seven gene expression matrix,

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.780
and these ten genes, also
below, are shown as plots.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:17.051
Can we cluster these genes into groups
of genes with the similar behavior?

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:23.160
Each row in the gene expression matrix
corresponds to a gene expression vector,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.250
and you can see the plot of this
gene expression vector at the bottom.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.750
And clearly, just looking
at this picture at the bottom

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.260
reveals three types of behavior:

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.400
Increasing expression, corresponding to
green genes; flat, corresponding to blue

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.180
genes; and red, corresponding to genes
whose expression is decreasing.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:55.630
And in fact, in 1997,
Joseph deRisi constructed a much larger

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:01.730
gene expression matrix by measuring
gene expression for 6,400 yeast genes,

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:07.420
nearly all yeast genes, before and
after the diauxic shift.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.445
And the goal he tried to achieve by
using this gene expression matrix

00:03:12.445 --> 00:03:17.420
was to partition all
yeast genes into clusters

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.940
so that genes in the same
cluster have similar behavior,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.485
and genes in different clusters
have different behavior.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:33.968
We can also represent genes as points
in multi-dimensional space, so

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:40.920
that an n x m gene expression matrix will
turn into n points in m-dimensional space,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.031
and our ten genes will
turn into these ten points,

00:03:46.031 --> 00:03:50.378
and we clearly see that blue points,

00:03:50.378 --> 00:03:54.961
whose expression remain
flat, cluster together.

00:03:54.961 --> 00:03:58.799
The same is true for
green points and red points.

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:03.755
However, I'm cheating here because
our points are actually points in

00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:09.547
seven-dimensional space and we measured
gene expression at seven checkpoints.

00:04:09.547 --> 00:04:14.000
But here, I show these points
in two-dimensional space.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.259
How have I done it?

00:04:15.259 --> 00:04:21.110
In fact, the clustering program is much
harder than it looks because there is so

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:25.490
called cursive dimensionality of studying

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:30.820
a clustering of points in
multi-dimensional spaces.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:36.370
So far, we talked about yeast gene expression,

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:43.290
but we can also talk about expression
of genes implicated in cancer.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:48.597
In 1999, Uri Alon measured
the expression of 2,000 genes

00:04:48.597 --> 00:04:53.817
from 40 samples of colon tumors
from cancer patients and

00:04:53.817 --> 00:04:58.863
compared it with the gene expression
matrix constructed for

00:04:58.863 --> 00:05:03.180
the same 2,000 genes for healthy patients.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.344
In this case,
the result is not one, but two,

00:05:07.344 --> 00:05:11.420
2000-by-40 gene expression matrices,

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:18.380
and the goal in this case is
to find genes with significantly

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:24.900
different expression vectors in tumor
patients as compared to healthy people.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.390
And these genes, if found, would represent

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:32.940
potential cancer biomarkers that
can be used for cancer diagnostics.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:38.630
So if we look at the
genes in a healthy patient

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:43.080
in multi-dimensional space, then for these,
let's say for ten, genes,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.500
let's not change the expression matrix,
we will see a picture like this.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.560
But if we superimpose it with
the expression levels for a cancer patients,

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:56.400
and I represent them by different a type of
circles, we see that for

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.260
blue genes, expression level
in cancerous patients and

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.360
in healthy patients are roughly the same.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:10.870
But in green genes and in red genes, they
differ and thus potentially green genes or

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.100
red genes represent potential,
I emphasize potential, cancer biomarkers.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.830
In fact, there are already
approaches to cancer diagnostics,

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:28.170
such as MammaPrint, that is
a test that evaluates the likelihood

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:33.660
of breast cancer recurrence based on
the expression levels of just 70 genes.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:40.580
The question that we will be interested
in is: How did scientists discover

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:46.060
these 70 human genes
implicated in breast cancer?

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.886
But to answer this question,

00:06:47.886 --> 00:06:51.545
we need to formally clustering
as an optimization problem.

